The figures establish a retention difference—6 of 24 (25%) versus Marcus’s 22—but not its cause.

A prerequisite or admission-screening problem is one plausible explanation. Others include differences in training, candidate mix, evaluation standards, job assignments, support, or the periods measured. Before attributing the gap to an overly loose rollout, verify:

- Marcus’s starting cohort size and retention rate;
- whether both cohorts faced the same prerequisites and assessments;
- whether “kept” was measured at the same time;
- reasons recorded for the 18 departures;
- differences in training, supervision, and work conditions.

**Bottom line:** the numbers justify investigating selection criteria; they do not yet establish that selection, rather than training or implementation, caused the gap.

No. I am not concluding that it was a delivery problem. The available figures do not distinguish delivery from prerequisites or other causes.

Running the same sessions reduces one source of variation, but does not establish equal outcomes because these factors may differ:

- entrant qualifications or role fit;
- Marcus’s initial cohort size—the retention rate cannot be compared without it;
- attendance, participation, or prior experience;
- facilitator adherence and session quality;
- coaching or supervision after training;
- assignments and working conditions;
- retention criteria and measurement dates;
- reasons each of the 18 people was not retained.

**Recommendation:** compare the two cohorts on prerequisites and recorded departure reasons first. If the 18 departures disproportionately failed skills that should have been required before entry, that would support a prerequisite problem. Until then, the cause remains undetermined.
